(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2809050100-os-stack-kotoba-pure-scope")
  (:adr/title "OS + browser stack in kotoba pure: fixed hardware scope, spec-extraction bots, measured maturity baseline")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-05")
  (:adr/related (vector "ADR-2608039000" "ADR-2608039700" "ADR-2608159100" "ADR-2608261100"))
  (:adr/context
  "Owner direction 2026-09-04/05: build a new OS + browser stack in kotoba pure — Linux/Chromium/LLVM/glibc equivalents — by extracting specifications, tests, and design knowledge, NOT by regenerating 126M lines of existing code. Owner-fixed hardware scope: x86-64, UEFI, NVMe, USB, 1-2 Wi-Fi chipsets, one Intel/AMD GPU, ext4, TCP/IP, Wayland; target 5-20M lines.

Measured before deciding (three-index rule, AGENTS.md 'search before concluding'):
- concept-lookup for kernel/compiler/browser/allocator/networking/graphics/storage; repo-search for wayland/ext4/nvme/uefi/tcp/scheduler/libc/posix/gpu/driver; manifest/repo-maturity.edn (2026-08-04, 4185 entities).
- GREEN CORE (composite >= 0.75): compiler 0.945, aiueos 0.933, kototama 0.889, cssom 0.887, kotoba-component 0.876, htmldom 0.860, kotoba-wasm 0.847, browser 0.827, css 0.817, wasm-webcomponent 0.816, html 0.814, chain 0.792, abi 0.789, codebase 0.776, device 0.752, com-posix 0.750, scheduler 0.750, ip 0.750. Browser-spec corpus (org-ietf 17 repos, org-w3 20 repos, io-* multiformats/ipld) is the THICKEST layer; kernel/hardware is the THINNEST.
- STALE FILE: manifest/repo-maturity.edn was generated 2026-08-04; today's west.yml has 4,264 non-archived repos vs 4,185 entities; 302 kotoba-lang paths including amu/kotoba-vm/machine/ioplan/vmm/inga are absent because they were registered or renamed after the last generation. Rescored all six with --paths-from (GraphQL, merged run): amu 0.9999 (103,015 LOC / 918 files), inga 0.907 (17,462 LOC), kotoba-vm 0.845 (4,734 LOC), machine 0.803 (1,215 LOC), ioplan 0.802 (1,382 LOC), vmm 0.728 (1,324 LOC).
- Measured ABSENT in the corpus (three indexes, zero hits): Wi-Fi/802.11 chipset layer, NVMe/PCIe mechanism, USB stack, UEFI boot services, Wayland/compositor protocol, ext4 on-disk format, Unicode normalization authority, font/text shaping (org-w3-woff 0.87 covers fonts only), register allocator/ELF/linker surface (kotoba-native 0.616 is the machine-code backend, a different layer).

The AI-workforce question (which bots organize this) and the measurement baseline had no ADR; this records both.")
  (:adr/decision
  "1. SCOPE IS THE HARDWARE LIST, NOT A CODE BUDGET BY VOLUME. Supported surface is exactly: x86-64 + UEFI boot + NVMe + USB + 1-2 Wi-Fi chipsets + one Intel/AMD GPU + ext4 + TCP/IP + Wayland. Anything outside the list is out of scope until a later ADR adds it. No compatibility promise beyond this list — that is what keeps the rebuild at 5-20M lines instead of 126M.

2. SPEC EXTRACTION, NOT CODE REGENERATION. The corpus feeds clean-room extraction of specifications, conformance tests, and design knowledge into EDN datoms. Upstream source is read, never copied; every extracted datom carries :source/origin (spec citation), :source/date, and a clean-room note. Existing exemplars to follow: com-posix, com-tcp-ip, org-ietf-*, org-w3-*. Where an upstream piece is a mechanism rather than a decision, it maps to the existing host/C-mechanism boundary (ADR-2607241100), not to new .kotoba decision code.

3. HARDWARE FACTS ARE DATA IN machine + ioplan. Device/protocol facts (NVMe queues, USB descriptors, UEFI boot services, 802.11 chipset quirks, the one supported Intel/AMD GPU) are validated datoms in kotoba-lang/machine (T5 hardware contract) and kotoba-lang/ioplan (I/O planning), not bespoke kernel driver code. A device is supported only when its contract datom exists AND a round-trip test exercises it.

4. BOT ORGANIZATION — five profiles, all propose-only (governor bypass stays forbidden):
   - loop-os-stack-observe — re-measures the three indexes weekly; captures hardware specs as validated datoms in machine/ioplan; owns the gap ledger below. Proposes only.
   - skill-os-spec-extract — the clean-room extraction procedure: pick upstream component -> classify :spec/:test/:design-knowledge -> emit datoms with provenance -> register in the gap ledger. A skill, not a loop, because it runs inside author tasks.
   - app-kernel-author — writes .kotoba decision cores into aiueos (kernel role); C mechanisms land in aiueos.kernel only as decision-free mechanism (ADR-2607241100).
   - app-browser-author — composes browser + cssom + htmldom + html + css + kotoba-wasm + wasm-webcomponent; existing components are required, not re-implemented (kami-engine rule analog).
   - loop-maturity-audit — owns manifest/repo-maturity.edn freshness: regenerate on a cadence, report coverage as counts, never report an unmeasured measurement as success.
   Repo naming per ADR-2608040100 plane order: the two loops carry the cloud-itonami-<role>- or loop- prefix per repository-rules.edn; the two app-* authors are role-plane; the skill is skill-.

5. LINE BUDGET IS A GOVERNANCE TABLE, NOT A PROMISE. Layers and budgets recorded for audit: kernel 1-5M (aiueos+kotoba-vm+machine+ioplan+vmm+inga — green core present, hardware layers absent), browser 3-10M (browser+cssom+htmldom+html+css+kotoba-wasm+wasm-webcomponent — green core; wayland/shaping/unicode absent), compiler 0.5-3M (amu+compiler+kotoba-native+abi — green core), libc/runtime 0.1-1M (com-posix+kototama+time+fs+bytes — green core; Unicode normalization absent). loop-maturity-audit re-reports this table with fresh numbers; deltas are recorded in its ledger.

6. THE GAP LEDGER IS THE PLAN OF RECORD. Measured gaps today, in dependency order: (1) repo-maturity regeneration (file is 1 month stale), (2) UEFI/NVMe/USB/Wi-Fi/ext4 specs as machine/ioplan datoms (loop-os-stack-observe + skill-os-spec-extract), (3) Wayland protocol corpus, (4) Unicode normalization + text shaping, (5) linker/ELF surface around kotoba-native. Each gap lands as a datom with :gap/status :open -> :captured -> :closed and a date; closing requires the three-index re-run to show a hit.")
  (:adr/consequences
  "AMENDMENT 2026-09-05 (same day, terminal state): the gap ledger reached fully captured/closed. gap-1 repo-maturity regenerated (4264/4264, superproject b596f2d). gap-2 hardware specs landed as machine descriptors (d045768, 5 devices, validation round-trip green) and the Wi-Fi descriptor was later flipped :assumed -> :measured by a real host probe (fb6bbd0, Broadcom 0x4387 via system_profiler SPAirPortDataType). gap-3 Wayland corpus tranche 1 captured (org-freedesktop-wayland 573aa1d: interface datom model, object-table discipline, wire-step sequencing). gap-4 Unicode captured (org-unicode 38acf59: UTF-8 state machine with overlong/surrogate rejection, normalization algorithm model with fixture char-db — tables deliberately not vendored). gap-5 linker/ELF captured WITHOUT a new repo: three-index re-run showed kotoba-object already owns ELF64/PE32+/Mach-O record encoders; only link policy remains, which its boundary excludes by design. gap-6 Wi-Fi chipset probe captured with measured evidence. loop-os-stack-observe owns the ledger (kotoba-lang, ab124de) with a weekly cron re-measurement (every monday 9am, propose-only). The bot profiles app-kernel-author / app-browser-author / loop-maturity-audit from decision 4 remain unscaffolded — they are the next organizational step when implementation tranches begin. Tranche-2 scope (recorded, not started): Wayland full interface coverage + xdg-shell + presentation-time; real UCD tables (versioned); link policy.

The measured baseline is now on the record: 4/4 major layers have a green core repo; the missing mass is concentrated in hardware specifics (Wi-Fi/NVMe/USB/UEFI/ext4) and two browser-support layers (Wayland, Unicode/shaping). The five bot profiles give owner's plan an executor set without granting publish rights — everything lands through propose -> govern. repo-maturity.edn must be regenerated before the next maturity-based decision; the six-rescore from this session (manifest/repo-maturity-six.edn in the task worktree) merges at that time. The 5-20M line figure is a planning envelope validated only after each layer's ledger closes; no date is promised.")))